<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  A1 — Android cleartext policy.

  Cleartext stays PERMITTED by default, and this is deliberate. Tailarr dials
  its OWN services over plain http:// to tailnet targets addressed two ways:

    * by MagicDNS name — the pairing transport is http://<controller>.ts.net:8089
      and the hidden gateway is http://tailarr-gate.<magicdns-suffix> (ntfy /
      services / notifications self-config). These ride cleartext INSIDE the
      WireGuard-encrypted tailnet tunnel, so they are safe, and they MUST keep
      working — a ts.net cleartext ban breaks pairing and gateway sync entirely.
    * by raw IP — non-HTTPS pods resolve to http://<tailnet-ip>:<port>
      (100.64.0.0/10 CGNAT) and hand-entered LAN services to http://192.168.x.x
      / http://10.x. Real Sonarr/Radarr/etc. live here.

  Android's static network-security-config can match domains but CANNOT
  range-match IPs, so a default cleartext ban (base cleartextTrafficPermitted=
  "false") would break every IP-addressed tailnet pod AND every hand-entered LAN
  service, not just untrusted public hosts. That is the "blanket ban" the A1
  finding explicitly forbids. A true public-only cleartext ban becomes possible
  in the tailnet-only tier (tracker note), where every service URL is
  https://*.ts.net and no IP-literal cleartext path remains — at that point the
  base flips to cleartextTrafficPermitted="false".

  This file replaces the deprecated android:usesCleartextTraffic manifest
  attribute with the modern, per-domain-capable mechanism so the tightening
  above is a one-line change when that tier ships.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
